{
  "_comment": "Authoritative machine config for the loop. Mirrors project.md prose; this file wins on conflict.",
  "mode": { "default": "goal" },
  "discovery": {
    "source": "local-goals",
    "_source_options": "local-goals (files in .sdlc/goals/) | github (issues labelled sdlc:goal, needs the gh CLI)",
    "order": "priority",
    "_order": "How the pending queue is ordered: \"priority\" (default) sorts by the goal's priority first and falls back to oldest-first within a tier; \"created\" is strict oldest-first, ignoring priority. Priority comes from the `priority:P0`..`priority:P4` label on GitHub, or the `priority:` frontmatter key on a local goal file. A goal with no priority (or an unrecognised one) sorts LAST, so a backlog that uses no priorities at all is ordered exactly as it was before this setting existed.",
    "reconcile": { "mode": "off", "ttl_minutes": 60, "open_issue_mode": "off" },
    "_reconcile": "#1391: the label/claim reconciliation sweep. 'off' (default) | 'on'. When on, next_batch runs ONE throttled pass per batch (never per pick) that surveys the backlog's label state and AUTOMATICALLY corrects exactly one class: a CLOSED issue still carrying sdlc:in-progress or a not-eligible overlay, which is inert today but becomes a live orphan the moment anyone reopens it. It touches nothing else unattended -- open-issue tangles are written to a proposal artifact for a human to approve (`reconcile.py propose`, then `reconcile.py apply --plan <path> --apply`). It refuses outright on an incomplete census, skips anything closed less than 24h ago, caps corrections per sweep, re-reads each issue immediately before writing, and never removes the goal label. ttl_minutes throttles the survey (it costs ~13 gh calls); a backlog's label state does not drift between two picks minutes apart. Run `reconcile.py census <dir>` any time for a read-only report.",
    "_reconcile_open_issue_mode": "#2295: a SEPARATE, independently off-by-default sub-gate -- 'off' (default) | 'on' -- for promoting DECISIVE open-issue MULTI_LABEL/ZERO_LABEL corrections (evidence: winner == the goal label, full stop, per the timeline oracle above) from the human-approved PROPOSED tier straight to AUTOMATIC. Deliberately NOT folded into reconcile.mode: that key already means something reviewed and narrower (closed issues only) on any repo that already has it 'on', so this ships its own switch rather than silently widening what an existing 'on' already does. Turning it on does not touch any correction whose winner is sdlc:parked or sdlc:needs-confirmation, or any overlay label (sdlc:in-progress, sdlc:blocked) -- those stay PROPOSED and human-reviewed exactly as before. Every promoted write gets a second, independent fresh eligibility re-check immediately before it happens (not just the label re-read every write here already gets), so a human acting on the same issue between the census and the write is never silently overridden. See docs/label-model.md section 5 and design #2287 for the full corrector-harm reasoning before flipping this to 'on'.",
    "auto_unpark": { "mode": "on" },
    "dependency_gate": { "mode": "on" },
    "_dependency_gate": "#1499: ON by default. Before a goal is CLAIMED, the picker reads the board mirror and checks the prerequisites the goal's own body declares (\"Blocked by: #N\", \"depends on #N\"). A goal whose prerequisite is still OPEN is left exactly as it was found -- sdlc:goal intact, no sdlc:in-progress, no claim, no comment -- and the pick moves to the next candidate; it becomes pickable by itself on the very next pick after the blocker closes. Without this the goal was claimed FIRST and found unworkable afterwards, which on a dependency-shaped backlog does not degrade, it stalls: every pick returns the same blocked goal and a human has to --skip each one. Costs nothing per pick whenever nothing is held (one local read of the board mirror; the mirror refetch behind it is TTL-guarded and shared with backlog_check). #1650: a pick that IS about to hold refreshes the mirror first rather than trusting a cache that ships with a 60-minute TTL -- two gh calls, at most once per pick however many goals are held, and never when the pick already fetched. If the board cannot be reached the hold stands and stderr says how old the evidence behind it is. Undeterminable dependency state always CLAIMS, and prints why. Set mode to \"off\" to switch it off repo-wide -- the escape for a body that says \"blocked by #N\" about something that is not really a dependency; the per-goal fix is to edit the body. github discovery mode only.",
    "_auto_unpark": "#1129/#1394: ON by default since #1394. Every pick re-examines each open sdlc:BLOCKED goal; if every \"blocked by #N\" it recorded now points at a CLOSED issue, the block is over, so sdlc:blocked is dropped and the goal returns to the queue -- and the card moves back to Ready on a board repo. That is the loop finishing its own work, not an extra feature, which is why opting OUT is the deliberate gesture: set mode to \"off\". It also keeps sdlc:blocking in sync on the blocker issues. A sdlc:PARKED issue is NEVER resumed by this sweep (#1394): the sweep decides on textual shape alone and cannot tell a stale block from a human's deliberate checkpoint, so a park stays human domain and /sigma-unpark is its route. Costs two issue-list calls per pick; github discovery mode only.",
    "blocking_priority_override": true,
    "_blocking_priority_override": "#1352/#1394: ON by default since #1394. An sdlc:goal issue that ALSO carries sdlc:blocking sorts ahead of every other pending issue, regardless of priority tier -- a structural override, not a priority nudge. On by default because an issue other work is BLOCKED on is the most valuable thing in the queue by definition: everything waiting on it is idle until it lands, so working it first is what makes a chain of blockers drain innermost-first instead of at whatever depth priority happens to pick. Set to false to turn the override off and let priority alone decide. Only the literal false disables it, so a typo lands on the default rather than silently changing how your queue is ordered. github discovery mode only.",
    "no_dangling_goal": { "enabled": false, "core": "" },
    "_no_dangling_goal": "#2263 (design #2253): OPT-IN 'no work sits outside the structure' -- a goal declaring NO unit of work at all (neither a body `Feature:` marker nor a `feature:*` label) is otherwise picked exactly as before this existed. enabled:true AND `.sdlc/features/` existing (both required -- applying this to an unadopted repo would set aside its entire backlog) turns that into one of two behaviours, chosen by `core`: left empty (the default once enabled), the goal is SET ASIDE -- it keeps sdlc:goal, gains the loud, self-clearing sdlc:needs-unit overlay (see docs/label-model.md §2b-iv), and resumes by itself the moment a human declares a unit, exactly the sdlc:needs-label shape for a different reason. Set `core` to a REGISTERED unit NAME (bootstrapped once via `/sigma-define`, the same way any other unit is opened -- checked clear of collisions on your own repo first, e.g. `gh label list --search core`) and an undeclared goal is run through the same 4-tier AI-judgment classifier every other unattributed goal reaches (#2363): a single existing-unit match, else this configured catch-all, else an evidence-backed unregistered-component set-aside, else `sdlc:needs-triage`. Attribution to the catch-all attaches a REAL `feature:<core>` label backed by a real branch and registry entry -- `core` is a genuine unit you can cut a branch from, not a sentinel or a comment-only marker. Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, changes nothing.",
    "github": {
      "repo": "",
      "goal_label": "sdlc:goal",
      "in_progress_label": "sdlc:in-progress",
      "parked_label": "sdlc:parked",
      "blocked_label": "",
      "_blocked_label": "Optional. A repo-local \"do not auto-pick\" label (spend gate, legal review, third-party wait) -- an issue carrying it is never returned by `next`/`next-batch`. Unset (empty, the default) changes nothing: this is a DIFFERENT label from parked_label above (which the loop itself applies when IT parks a goal) -- you set/clear blocked_label by hand, the loop only ever reads it. Applies on BOTH queue paths: _fetch_pending excludes it on the label queue, and _card_is_eligible excludes it on the board queue -- labels decide eligibility, the board decides only order. A `Blocked` board column is complementary (it moves the card where a human can see it), never a substitute (it does not gate the pick). /sigma-doctor warns if it finds a blocked-looking label on your issues while this stays unset.",
      "goal_blocked_label": "sdlc:blocked",
      "_needs_label_label": "OVERLAY (#1468). Rides alongside sdlc:goal on a goal whose body declares a feature: unit whose LABEL does not exist on this repo yet. Visible to every sweep, picked by nothing. NOT sdlc:blocked -- that one means waiting on another ISSUE and is cleared by the auto-unpark sweep when the blocker closes; this one is cleared by Sigma itself as soon as a human creates the label, and /sigma-unpark neither lists nor applies to it.",
      "needs_label_label": "sdlc:needs-label",
      "_needs_unit_label": "OVERLAY (#2263). The sibling of needs_label_label immediately above -- SAME shape, DIFFERENT reason: rides alongside sdlc:goal on a goal that declares NO unit at all (neither a body Feature: marker nor a feature:* label), rather than one whose label is missing. Only ever written when discovery.no_dangling_goal.enabled is true AND .sdlc/features/ exists (see that key's own comment); this label never appears on an adopter who has not opted in. Visible to every sweep, picked by nothing, cleared by Sigma itself the moment a human declares a unit.",
      "needs_unit_label": "sdlc:needs-unit",
      "_goal_blocked_label": "#1350/#1393: DISTINCT from blocked_label immediately above -- that one is a human-set, read-only \"do not auto-pick\" convention (#1205) the loop never writes. This one IS code-managed: an in-progress goal that discovers a genuine blocking dependency (handoff.py's blocks_goal=True) gets it immediately. It is an OVERLAY, not a state of its own -- #1393: the goal KEEPS goal_label and only loses in_progress_label, so goal+blocked is the correct, normal shape of a blocked goal. Membership is kept because every sweep, census, mirror and reclaim path queries --label goal_label; an issue that drops it is findable only by whichever query happens to ask for the overlay, and one lost label makes it an orphan. Eligibility does not depend on the removal: next_pending and the board's own card check both exclude this label unconditionally (always-on, no config gate), so a blocked goal is visible to everything and picked by nothing. Only a PARK gives up membership. Always on; there's no reason to turn this off, only to rename it if it collides with a pre-existing label on your repo.",
      "blocking_label": "sdlc:blocking",
      "_blocking_label": "#1351: DERIVED and self-healing -- applied to the BLOCKER issue itself (not the blocked goal goal_blocked_label marks above), for as long as at least one open issue still references it via a live \"Blocked by #N\" marker; auto-removed once the last such reference resolves. Also DISTINCT from the static, one-time sdlc:dependency tag (handoff.py's DEPENDENCY_LABEL, applied once at cross-area filing time and never updated) -- this one is reconciled continuously by auto_unpark.py's own sweep, whenever discovery.auto_unpark.mode is \"on\". #1393: MEMBERSHIP travels with this label. Something other work waits on is by definition waiting to be PICKED, so wherever sdlc:blocking is applied goal_label is applied with it -- this label is only a TIE-BREAK among issues that are ALREADY eligible (it re-ranks when discovery.blocking_priority_override is on), never a queue of its own, so without membership a blocker carrying it would be picked by nothing. Three exceptions, where membership is not Sigma's to grant: a PARKED blocker (human domain -- only /sigma-unpark restores it), a proposal a HUMAN filed (that is a real decision), and a plain third-party issue (never ours to adopt); those get the label as information and are surfaced for a person. blockers.py applies the same policy at the moment a block is recorded (Sigma's own unapproved follow-up is promoted; one belonging to someone else is granted membership and recorded in the ledger for them; a human-filed proposal, a parked blocker and a third-party issue are surfaced with the route out named, never touched), and /sigma-doctor reports whatever residue is left. Always on; there's no reason to turn this off, only to rename it if it collides with a pre-existing label on your repo.",
      "priority_label_prefix": "priority:",
      "_priority_label_prefix": "The label prefix priority is spelled with, for `order: priority`. Change it if your repo already uses its own severity scheme (e.g. \"sev:\") — otherwise every one of those issues ranks as unprioritised.",
      "assignee": null,
      "_assignee": "Optional. Set to \"@me\" (or a username) to only pick issues assigned to that user, so several people can run the loop on one shared board without grabbing each other's work. null (this scaffold's own default) means NOT YET DECIDED -- /sigma-setup fills this in with \"@me\" the first time it adopts a github repo (#2255); it is inert regardless in local-goals mode, and a bare /sigma-init with no /sigma-setup afterward behaves exactly as an empty filter (every open goal issue in scope) either way. An explicit empty string \"\" is a DIFFERENT, deliberate choice -- \"no filter, on purpose\" -- and, like any other real value here, is never overwritten by /sigma-setup on a later rerun.",
      "project": {
        "enabled": true,
        "_comment": "Mirror issue status onto a GitHub Projects v2 board (needs the gh `project` scope). Flow: Backlog -> Ready -> In Progress -> QC -> Done, with Blocked for a machine block and Parked for a human park. Fail-open: if it can't, the loop still runs on issues+labels.",
        "priority_field": "Priority",
        "_priority_field": "Name of the P0-P4 single-select column sigma provisions on a board it CREATES. It is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for priority, mirroring how the `Ready` column is the source of truth for the queue: set a card's Priority on the board and the loop ranks by it, then brings the `priority:P*` LABEL into line so issue search and local mode agree. A card whose Priority is blank falls back to its label, and a board with no Priority column falls back to labels entirely - so the field's absence is the gate and no config is needed to keep the old behaviour. Ready cards are ordered (priority, board position): tiers first, your drag order within a tier. Set false to create and write nothing. An existing board never gets the field from the loop; add it with board_migrate.py.",
        "archive_done": true,
        "_archive_done": "Archive a card once its goal completes, so the board stays close to one page instead of growing without bound (a mature board is mostly Done cards, which is noise for anyone reading it). Strictly opt-in and strictly the boolean true - archiving is user-visible and not conveniently reversible in bulk, so an absent key archives nothing and an existing board is never tidied behind your back. Fail-open: failing to archive never fails a goal that completed.",
        "queue_source": "status",
        "_queue_source": "Which representation DECIDES what is queued. \"status\" (default): the board's `Ready` column is the source of truth - a card in Ready is the current sprint and will be picked, in board order, so dragging a card reorders the queue; the `sdlc:goal` label is still written but never decides. \"label\": the historical rule, the label alone decides. This setting is only an escape hatch - the real gate is structural: a board with NO `Ready` option cannot be a queue, so an existing board keeps behaving exactly as it did until you migrate it deliberately. Nothing here ever adds `Ready` to a board sigma did not create.",
        "owner": "",
        "title": "",
        "number": null,
        "_number": "PIN THIS to your board's number if a board already exists. sigma resolves a board by `number`, else by exact `title`, else by its default title `<repo> — SDLC`; if none matches it CREATES one. With no number set and a real board titled differently, that create would be a silent DUPLICATE the loop then manages instead of yours - so if the owner already has board(s) sigma now REFUSES to create and warns instead (set this). Leave null only for a fresh repo with no board yet. /sigma-doctor flags the risk.",
        "status_field": "Status",
        "columns": { "backlog": "Backlog", "ready": "Ready", "in_progress": "In Progress", "qc": "QC", "done": "Done", "blocked": "Blocked", "parked": "Parked" },
        "_columns": "#1391 step 3a: `ready` was MISSING from this map even though it names the single most consequential column in the whole file — under the default `queue_source: \"status\"` the `Ready` lane IS the pick queue (`_ready_lane`/`_board_queue`), and its absence is also the backward-compatibility gate for every adopter whose board has no such lane. The code has always defaulted it to \"Ready\", so nothing was broken; but an adopter whose board names that lane something else had no key here to say so, and could not tell from this file that the lane governed anything. Rename it here to match your board; the other names work the same way. `parked` was added the same way and for the same reason: a park and a machine block are DIFFERENT states — a park is a human checkpoint only /sigma-unpark reopens, a block clears itself the moment its blocker closes — but both used to write the `blocked` column, so on a board carrying both options the card contradicted the labels. A park now writes `parked` and FALLS BACK to `blocked` when the board has no such option (costing no gh call to discover that), so an adopted board predating it behaves exactly as before; a board this kit CREATES gets the option provisioned.",
        "custom_fields": {},
        "_custom_fields": "Values sigma stamps on your board's CUSTOM single-select fields for any issue IT creates (today: cross-area hand-offs), so a loop-made issue isn't silently blank on a field like Priority/Section while every human-made issue carries it. Map field NAME -> option NAME, e.g. {\"Priority\": \"Medium\", \"Section\": \"Task\"}. ONLY single-select fields are settable (like the built-in Status); a field the board lacks, or a value that isn't one of its options, is skipped - not guessed. Empty {} = the historical behavior (sigma sets only labels + assignee + the built-in Status). NOTE a `priority:<n>` LABEL is a different mechanism from a Projects-v2 Priority FIELD; this maps the field. /sigma-doctor lists any single-select field you left unmapped."
      }
    }
  },
  "budget": { "max_iterations": 20, "max_tokens": 100000000, "max_codex_raw_tokens": 0, "max_minutes": 480 },
  "_budget_codex": "Set budget.max_codex_raw_tokens to a positive per-run measured Codex phase input+output ceiling after observing your own workload. Zero disables it. Cached input is included. This is checked before the next goal admission; the orchestrator and unavailable rollouts are not counted, so it is not an account quota. Codex and Claude token units are deliberately separate.",
  "handoff": { "enabled": true, "after_goals": 20 },
  "_handoff": "#2521: bounds the ORCHESTRATING session's own context, independent of budget.max_tokens above (which Claude phase reports feed only when the entire phase is priceable; Codex pricing is unavailable). Every after_goals-th goal, loop.py's own `next`/`next-batch` stops the run with `LOOP STOP: handoff` instead of returning the next goal, exactly the way a budget stop already does -- supervise.sh relaunches a fresh session on it automatically (same short 60s pause budget gets), and an unsupervised in-session run is told the literal command to run next. ON by default and enabled at 20 EVEN WHEN THIS WHOLE KEY IS ABSENT -- unlike every other opt-in block in this file, an existing install's own already-on-disk config.json predates this key by construction (templates are applied once, at /sigma-init time, never re-synced), so an absent-means-off reading would leave an existing install exhibiting unbounded per-session growth forever. Set enabled:false to opt back into the old, unbounded-single-session behaviour once you have measured your own repo does not need this. 20 matches budget.max_iterations's own already-shipped default -- same order of magnitude, deliberately not a freshly re-derived number; tune after_goals down for a smaller context budget per session, or up if a fresh subagent's own ~70-77k-token starting cost outweighs the benefit on your backlog. NAMING NOTE: unrelated to this file's OTHER `ledger.handoff`/`_handoff` key further below (a cross-area blocker escalation -- see handoff.py) -- same word, two different mechanisms, no functional collision, but grep-worthy to know both exist.",
  "gates": {
    "hard_plan_gate": { "enabled": false, "plan_freshness_hours": 24 },
    "stop_gate": { "enabled": false, "plan_freshness_hours": 24 },
    "_stop_gate": "OPT-IN Stop hook (completion_gate.sh). When enabled:true, refuses to let the agent STOP with unplanned SOURCE changes in the working tree (no fresh plan under .sdlc/plans/ within plan_freshness_hours) — the Stop-time counterpart to hard_plan_gate. Catches an interactive /sigma-goal session ending early; the loop's own record step is already guarded. Off by default (installing changes nothing). Escape hatch: touch .sdlc/.allow-direct-edits. Fail-open on no python3/git/config.",
    "plan_review": { "enabled": false },
    "_plan_review": "OPT-IN, host-agnostic (plain Python in `work.py pr`; needs work.enabled). When enabled:true, `work.py pr` refuses to push unless `work.py record-plan-review` recorded an approving plan-review verdict (SOUND or SOUND-WITH-REFINEMENTS) for the exact bytes (sha256) of the goal's plan .sdlc/plans/<stem>.md as it is on the branch (the main checkout's copy when the branch carries none) -- edit the plan after its review and pr refuses until a fresh plan-review is recorded. Checked at `work.py pr` only: a later `work.py rebase` force-push and `merge()` do not re-check. Covers the plan .md only: not a .slices.json manifest, not a design PR; a goal with no plan is left to hard_plan_gate. Not org-lockable. The record is agent-written: it proves a verdict was recorded for these bytes, not that an independent reviewer produced it. Off by default.",
    "phase_record": { "enabled": true },
    "_phase_record": "#684: host-agnostic Python gate (`loop.py record done` in every mode, and `work.py merge`). A goal is not done until the action log shows research, plan, an approved plan-review, implement (started after it), an approved review and retro, each dispatched as its own subagent and bracketed with phase_report.py start/end. The refusal names each missing item and its fix. Lever for a trivial goal: `loop.py waive-phases <sdlc> <goal> research,retro --reason \"<why>\"` (recorded and visible; plan, plan-review, implement and review are never waivable). `enabled: false` turns the whole gate off; it also needs action_log.enabled true (it refuses rather than pass unverified). `loop.py phases <sdlc> <goal>` prints what is recorded and the next step. Proves boundaries and verdicts were recorded in order, not that the work was good.",
    "_decision_gate": "PreToolUse hook (decision_gate.py) — the one guardrail here that is NOT a prompt: an edit that assigns a registered invariant a violating value is DENIED, not discouraged, when it is written in a shape the gate reads (`name = <literal>` or `name: <literal>`; `self.name = 99` and an annotated `name: int = 99` are NOT matched, so `decision_gate.py check .` is the backstop). Unlike the other gates there is no `enabled: true` to set — AUTHORING .sdlc/decisions.json is the opt-in, so no registry means no behavior. Run /sigma-decide to write one, then `decision_gate.py check .` to see whether the code you already have complies. Add `\"decision_gate\": {\"enabled\": false}` here only to switch it OFF without deleting the registry (a refactor that intentionally moves an invariant). /sigma-doctor reports how many decisions are active. The hook needs an adopted repo (this file); `decision_gate.py init` refuses without it.",
    "irreversible_actions": ["deploy", "delete", "overwrite", "spend", "migrate"],
    "on_block": "park"
  },
  "decision_tier": "off",
  "_decision_tier": "OPT-IN, advisory-only park-detail classifier (decision_tier.py). \"off\" (default): every park behaves exactly as it did before this key existed. \"auto\": a park whose reason_class is needs_decision, irreversible, or unknown (i.e. carries real judgment-call text -- see decision_tier.py's own docstring) is classified into autonomous/escalate_l1/escalate_l0, per #818's L2/L1/L0 escalation pyramid; the tier is surfaced in the ledger `park` event, the `- tier:` line in .sdlc/state/review-queue.md, and the park comment, so a human triaging the queue can see severity at a glance. Advisory only: it never changes what the loop DOES with a parked goal -- routing every park to a human regardless of tier is a separate, larger feature (tracked apart from this classifier) and is not implemented by this flag. /sigma-doctor reports the live state.",
  "verify": { "command": "", "enforce": false },
  "_verify": "The proving command `loop.py verify` runs, and whether `record done` requires it green. The command is read from TWO places only: a local goal's frontmatter `verify_command` (wins), else `verify.command` here -- nothing else (.sdlc/project.md is not read for it). #228: enforce ON with an EMPTY command refuses every `done` forever (verify prints NO-COMMAND, exit 3; record done REFUSED, exit 4), so this template ships enforce OFF and /sigma-init writes the reason into verify._why. Enforce turns ON when a command is confirmed: /sigma-init detects a candidate (pytest, package.json scripts.test, go.mod, Cargo.toml, a Makefile test target, a CI test step) and `python3 <installed-sigma>/skills/sigma-init/scripts/verify_detect.py confirm .sdlc <n> <id>` records candidate n with enforce ON only if it still has the id printed beside it (it re-reads the repo and refuses if the repo changed, so no repository text is pasted into a shell and what is stored is what was shown; `set .sdlc --command-file <file>` records your own); `... decline .sdlc` keeps enforce OFF and says why. /sigma-doctor flags enforce ON with no command as a failing check.",
  "session_start": { "enabled": false },
  "_session_start": "OPT-IN SessionStart hook (session_start.sh). When enabled:true, injects a short SDLC policy brief + a doctor-lite install self-check as additionalContext at the start of every session, so Sigma's conventions are in context before the first prompt (the UserPromptSubmit gate only fires once you type). Advisory only — never blocks; the self-check warns. Off by default: silent unless a .sdlc/ exists AND this is true. Fail-open on no python3/config.",
  "work": {
    "enabled": true,
    "_comment": "One git worktree + branch + PR per goal, so the loop never moves your checkout - and never rewrites .sdlc/goals/ underneath itself by switching branches. The worktree is cut fresh from <remote>/<base>, which IS the goal-start rebase: nothing to replay, so it cannot conflict or strand a half-applied tree. verify_command then runs IN that worktree, because the main checkout does not contain the change. #2741: ships true directly from /sigma-init as of 1.0.0 (was null/NOT-YET-DECIDED, decided by /sigma-setup on first github adoption per #2255) -- a fresh install now gets a worktree/branch/PR per goal from the first run. Set explicit false to keep the loop writing to git exactly as little as before. Gitignore .sdlc/work/.",
    "base": "",
    "_base": "The integration branch to cut from and target. Empty = whichever branch the loop was started on. GITHUB-BACKED GOALS ONLY (discovery.source == \"github\", and a goal whose id is an issue number): this is then the FALLBACK, not the only answer -- a goal whose ISSUE declares a unit of work (a `feature:<name>` label, or a `Feature: <name>` line in the body) is cut from `feature/<name>` instead, resolved per goal. In local-goals mode nothing is read and no marker in a .sdlc/goals/*.md file has any effect, so this key is always the answer. A goal declaring no unit uses this key too, exactly as it always did.",
    "remote": "origin",
    "branch_prefix": "sdlc/",
    "worktree_dir": ".sdlc/work",
    "allow_secret_paths": [],
    "_allow_secret_paths": "#1555: `work.py commit` stages with `git add -A` and REFUSES the commit when what git actually staged includes a secret-shaped NAME (.env and its variants, *.pem/*.key/*.p12/*.pfx/*.jks/*.keystore, id_rsa/id_dsa/id_ecdsa/id_ed25519, credentials.json, client_secret*.json, *service-account*.json, serviceAccountKey.json, .netrc, .pgpass, .htpasswd). Names only - no file is ever read, and high-entropy content scanning is deliberately NOT done here. The PUBLIC half of a keypair is never refused: *.pub, and a `cert`/`fullchain`/`chain`/`ca`/`public` token in a .pem stem (or `public` in a .key stem) means the file is meant to be committed - `ca.pem` passes, `ca.key` does not. A staged DELETION is never refused either, so the goal that REMOVES a leaked credential is exactly the one this never blocks. It fails CLOSED, unlike the plan check in the same file, because the harms are not symmetric: a refusal costs an operator minutes and leaves every file untouched, while a pushed credential is in a PR, in forks and in caches and can only be rotated. `git add -A` honours .gitignore, so a repo that already ignores its `.env` never sees this at all - and /sigma-doctor reports which secret-shaped files are still unignored BEFORE the loop ever commits. This key is the escape hatch for the deliberate case (a test key, a fixture cert, a Symfony-style non-secret .env, a Keynote deck named *.key): list the EXACT repo-relative path of each file that may be committed. Exact paths only - globs are not expanded, on purpose, because [\"*\"] would be an off switch wearing an allowlist's clothes. The refusal names this key and prints the paths ready to paste.",
    "rebase_upkeep": "on",
    "_rebase_upkeep": "on (default) | off. Keeps a long-lived `feature/<name>` branch current: on every pick of a goal that declares a unit, the feature branch is rebased onto `base` and the unit's goal worktrees are then replayed onto it, so an in-flight unit reflects what has landed instead of meeting it as one wall of conflicts at the end. Conflicts are filed as tracked issues rather than left half-applied -- either level aborts and leaves both the checkout and the remote exactly as they were. Force-pushes the feature branch, which is safe ONLY because nobody commits to it directly (all work arrives through `sdlc/*` goal branches); the pass MEASURES that before it pushes and refuses when it finds a commit no pull request accounts for. Requires merge_method squash or merge -- rebase leaves no trace of the PR on the landed commits, so the check has no evidence and the pass declines. A unit with no feature branch, and a goal declaring no unit, are no-ops. The pass replays a unit's sdlc/* worktrees only where it can PROVE nobody is working them (a dead agent marker or an expired claim); with no evidence it leaves the branch to its own owner, whose merge path already rebases it when GitHub reports the PR BEHIND. It rebases in a throwaway worktree under .sdlc/state/rebase/<unit> and clears a stale one on entry -- but turning this off immediately after a crash leaves that worktree registered, so clear it by hand with `git worktree remove --force .sdlc/state/rebase/<unit> && git worktree prune`.",
    "auto_merge": "off",
    "_auto_merge": "off (default, never merges - the reviewed PR is left for a human and the goal is recorded `review` until it merges; `record done` is refused while the PR is unmerged) | protected (merge only where branch protection actually REQUIRES checks or reviews - i.e. delegate the gate to GitHub) | always (merge whenever clean+safe, even on an unprotected branch, where nothing but the local verify gated it). Legacy booleans still parse: false=off, true=always.",
    "_auto_merge_2": "Whatever the setting, a merge needs BOTH this run's fresh passing verify evidence (whenever verify is required: verify.enforce on, or a verify command declared) AND GitHub's mergeable + mergeStateStatus CLEAN, and it then lands with a direct `gh pr merge`; GitHub's own --auto is armed only for a required check that has not answered yet. A BEHIND branch is rebased once and re-checked; anything else parks with the reason.",
    "_auto_merge_3": "PERMISSION is never a config question: a fork PR, or a repo you only have read/triage access to, opens the PR and stops there - that is the open-source path, where the PR IS the deliverable. It records `review` (awaiting merge), not a park; the goal becomes `done` - and its issue closes - only once the PR is observed merged (done means merged).",
    "_auto_merge_4": "The Stage-1 design-PR merge on goal-review CONFIRM (`merge-design`) is governed by `work.enabled` alone, not by this setting.",
    "merge_method": "squash",
    "unit_completion": "surface",
    "_unit_completion": "#1478 (branching model): what Sigma does when a UNIT of work — every issue carrying one `feature:<name>` label — has no open issues left. \"surface\" (DEFAULT): say so on stderr and leave one ledger note addressed to the unit's owner, e.g. `unit 'int-contract' looks complete — 0 open issues, 12 closed`. It opens NO pull request and merges NOTHING. \"draft-pr\": additionally open the `feature/<name>` -> `work.base` landing pull request AS A DRAFT and park it — a draft is unmergeable on GitHub, so the draft itself is the park; readying, reviewing and merging it stay yours. It never re-raises a landing PR a human already closed or merged -- the read is state=all precisely so it can see that decision instead of opening a fresh draft over it. \"off\": not even the measurement, zero calls. Sigma NEVER decides a unit is finished and NEVER merges a feature branch in any mode: \"no open issues\" is not \"the work is done\", so a person raises that PR and a person reviews it. Only the exact literals \"draft-pr\" and \"off\" select the non-defaults — every other value (a typo, a boolean, \"draft\") lands on \"surface\", because nothing should reach the mode that writes to your repo by misspelling something else. Inert with no .sdlc/features/ (the branching model unadopted) and on any goal not cut from a feature/<name> base, at zero API calls. Every call it makes is REST (`gh api repos/<slug>/...`), never the GraphQL-backed `gh issue list` / `gh pr create` -- GitHub meters the two on separate hourly budgets and #1209 moved PR creation off GraphQL for exactly that reason. One REST call per goal that finishes on a unit (one page at REST's per_page ceiling; the count and its truncation flag both describe ISSUES, since that endpoint returns pull requests too and the page cap bounds both together); the goal that CLOSES one also reads the closed tally and, for a cross-repo unit, #1474's both-green sibling check — which is REPORTED on that line, never acted on.",
    "require_review": "changes",
    "reclaim_merged_worktrees": false,
    "_reclaim_merged_worktrees": "#465: true lets `loop.py start` and `loop.py record ... done` run one bounded sweep (3 removals, 8 GitHub REST reads, 20 s, at most every 15 minutes) that removes the checkout of a goal worktree whose PR is positively merged, with every commit in the tree in that PR, a clean tree and nothing alive owning it; never a branch or the work record. Default false because the proof costs REST quota. The operator lever always works: `python3 skills/sigma-loop/scripts/worktree_prune.py list-removable .sdlc`.",
    "max_review_cycles": 3,
    "_max_review_cycles": "Hard cap on the loop's own review->fix->re-review loop (require_review on). Each `sigma:block` the loop posts counts a cycle; at this many it stops asking for more and PARKS the goal for a human instead of churning forever if the review keeps finding new problems. Enforced in `work.py post-review`, not left to prose. A value below 1 (0 included) is NOT \"no cap\": it falls back to the default of 3.",
    "_require_review": "off | changes (default, #2741 -- was off) | approval. A REAL PR-review gate, independent of branch protection. `gate()`'s mergeStateStatus only folds in reviews the base branch PROTECTION requires, so a human 'Request changes' on an unprotected base (a common staging/dev shape) is invisible to auto-merge. `changes` parks on a CHANGES_REQUESTED review or an unresolved review thread; `approval` also requires an APPROVED reviewDecision before merging. Self-review before the PR is never enough on its own - this is the review AFTER the PR. Adds a `gh pr view` read per merge; fail-open on a read error. Note: this key does NOT take a review MECHANISM (there is no \"subagent\" value here -- an unrecognised string silently normalises to off, see work.py's normalise_review_mode); the mechanism a review actually runs by (subagent on Claude, the resolver's own fallback on Codex) is `review.host` below, already \"auto\" by default and unrelated to this key.",
    "_require_review_2": "THE LOOP REVIEWS ITS OWN PR - no human required. This is the READ side of the gate; the WRITE side is `work.py post-review`: after opening the PR, the loop runs a fresh adversarial pass over the real diff and posts `sigma:approve` (clears the merge) or `sigma:block` (sends itself back to fix + re-review). GitHub forbids formally approving your OWN PR and the loop is always the author, so a plain comment is the channel: sigma:approve/block/unblock, latest wins, a block overrides an approve. A marker counts only from an OWNER, MEMBER or COLLABORATOR commenter (GitHub authorAssociation); anyone else on a public repo is ignored with a stderr note, and unreadable comments park the merge. The same markers let a human review a loop PR too; a formal APPROVE / unresolved thread also counts when a second identity leaves one. Drive it via /sigma-loop."
  },
  "upkeep": {
    "enabled": false,
    "units": { "include": ["*"], "exclude": [] },
    "triggers": { "drift_merges": "auto", "every_hours": 24, "min_interval_minutes": 120, "dormant_days": 14, "dormant_every_hours": 72 },
    "auto": { "window_days": 21, "burst_window_hours": 24, "target_hours": 12, "floor": 3, "ceiling": 40 },
    "verify": { "clean_rebase": false, "timeout_minutes": 60 },
    "backup": { "keep_days": 14, "keep_last": 5, "former_prefixes": [] },
    "conflicts": { "resolve": "off", "mechanical_without_verify": false }
  },
  "_upkeep": "OFF BY DEFAULT: settings for automatic, optional upkeep of long-lived feature branches (a schedule, a backup of the old tip when a unit branch is rewritten, an upgraded rebase pass). In this release the block is read in three places, and only once `upkeep.enabled` is true: (1) when the pick-time rebase pass force-pushes a unit branch it also keeps the old tip as a backup ref (under the backup namespace, in the same atomic push, so both land or neither does), and refuses a unit whose name is too long for that ref; (2) `feature_rebase.py restore` puts a unit branch back at one of its backup refs and `feature_rebase.py prune` removes backup refs older than `backup.keep_days` while keeping the newest `backup.keep_last` of each unit. Nothing prunes by itself yet, so run the prune now and then while upkeep is enabled. (3) the upkeep pass (feature_upkeep_pass.py) skips a unit that has already landed in its base - its tip is contained in the base, or equals the head of a merged landing request - and records the skip, and never moves a landed unit; the pass also keeps a per-machine file of recomputed acknowledgements after a rewrite. While enabled, a goal cut from a unit branch records the unit tip it was cut from, so a later goal rebase replays only the goal's own commits onto a rewritten unit, and the attended rebase door takes the unit's lock and pushes with a lease on the exact tip it saw. `backup.former_prefixes` ships empty; list a namespace here (the form refs/<name>/backup/) only if an earlier product wrote backups there and you want them pruned too. NOTHING ELSE RUNS FROM THIS BLOCK YET: no schedule or resolver exists, so the other keys have no effect today. THREE SEPARATE OPT-INS will be needed and all default to off: (1) the project - `upkeep.enabled: true` in this file, the JSON boolean true only (the text \"true\", the text \"yes\" and the number 1 all stay OFF); (2) the machine - the environment variable SIGMA_UPKEEP_JOB set to exactly 1, plus `ledger.enabled: true`, before any background job may run; (3) spend, which a later release adds. THE GATE FAILS CLOSED: a wrong type, a number out of range, a misspelt or unknown key, or a pair that contradicts itself (`auto.floor` above `auto.ceiling`; the triggers must satisfy min_interval_minutes <= every_hours*60 <= dormant_every_hours*60) turns the WHOLE block off, and the reason names the key. Keys that start with an underscore are notes and are ignored. Every number is a whole number of at least 1. NOT THE SAME AS `work.rebase_upkeep`: that older switch (on by default) governs the rebase that runs when a goal is picked, and an unrecognised value there reads as ON. This block is the opposite - off by default, and an unrecognised value reads as OFF. `work.rebase_upkeep: \"off\"` still turns the pick-time pass off whatever this block says. Projects scaffolded earlier do not receive this block (the scaffold never overwrites an existing config); copy it in if you want it.",
  "_upkeep_conflicts": "RESERVED, OFF BY DEFAULT: the `upkeep.conflicts` keys, read only by the upkeep gate (feature_upkeep.py) and only once `upkeep.enabled` is true. `resolve`: \"off\" (default) leaves a conflict in a unit rebase exactly as it is today; \"mechanical\" and \"agent\" are the levels later releases add, and in this release neither changes what runs. Any other text, in any case, is a typo and turns the WHOLE upkeep block off with a reason naming the key (the gate fails closed). `mechanical_without_verify`: false (default); a later release reads it to decide whether a mechanical resolution may skip the verify step.",
  "decision_rubric": {
    "records": { "enabled": false, "repo_visibility": "private", "store_dir": "decisions", "lock_timeout_s": 20, "archive_after_days": 180, "max_files_warn": 5000 }
  },
  "_decision_rubric": "OFF BY DEFAULT: the decision rubric (recorded decisions, earned trust levels, one waiting state for AI-filed issues). One sub-block per part; each part opens only when its own `enabled` is the JSON boolean true (the text \"true\", \"yes\" and the number 1 all stay OFF). This release reads `records.enabled`, `records.repo_visibility` and the store keys (`store_dir`: a plain folder name under the state directory where one file per decision is committed, default decisions; `lock_timeout_s`: seconds to wait for the index lock before a rebuild is skipped, default 20; `archive_after_days`: age at which the person-run archive verb folds records into a yearly file, default 180; `max_files_warn`: the doctor warns above this many record files, default 5000; a wrong-typed or out-of-range value reads as its default). Records describe the repository and its areas, never a person. Visibility (\"private\" default | \"public\"; any other value reads as private; set it to public when the repository is public so records follow the public-repository rules). A wrong-typed block, part or `enabled` closes the WHOLE block and /sigma-doctor names the key. Keys that start with an underscore are notes. Projects scaffolded earlier do not receive this block; copy it in to opt in.",
  "ai_filed": {
    "triage": { "enabled": true },
    "buckets": { "urgent": "P2", "routine": "P3", "unclear": "P4" }
  },
  "_ai_filed": "Triage of AI-filed issues (follow-ups, upkeep findings). PROVISIONAL defaults, all overridable. `triage.enabled` (default true; the JSON boolean false switches it off and restores plain queueing; any other non-true value parks). A filing is checked against a deny-list first (hard-stop words and shapes, secret shapes, a recorded-permission marker on the first body line) which can only park, then mapped to a bucket whose priority comes from `buckets` (urgent P2, routine P3, unclear P4). Every bucket must rank strictly below the human default `human_default_priority` (P1, optional key); a bucket at or above it, or not P0-P4, parks every filing with a named reason. Optional `triage.deny_words` replaces the default word list. Priorities are never blocking and the decision never depends on who filed. Library only in this release (file_triage.py): nothing calls it yet. Projects scaffolded earlier do not receive this block; copy it in.",
  "ledger": {
    "enabled": null,
    "_comment": "Append-only team record of what the loop did: .sdlc/ledger/entries/<actor>-<host>.<pid>.jsonl, ONE FILE PER WRITING PROCESS (you only ever write your own, so concurrent appends cannot conflict - the instance token, not just the actor, since several parallel loops can share one login and several machines can share one bot account). EXPECT MANY FILES PER ACTOR: every run adds one, nothing compacts them, and hundreds for a single actor is normal - nothing is read back by filename, so the count is harmless. The team view is their union - render it with `ledger.py render <dir> --write`. null (this scaffold's own default) means NOT YET DECIDED -- /sigma-setup turns this on the first time it adopts a github repo (#2255), since a real team adoption wants the shared record; a bare /sigma-init with no /sigma-setup afterward, or an explicit false, both behave OFF -- the loop behaves exactly as before either way.",
    "actor": "",
    "_actor": "Your handle in the ledger. Empty = resolved from the authenticated account (`gh api user`), else the shell user.",
    "owners": {},
    "_owners": "Optional area -> owner overrides, e.g. {\"engine\": \"@some-login\"}. Empty = the roster is the repo's own .github/CODEOWNERS, which the host already enforces on every PR.",
    "handoff": { "label": "sdlc:dependency" },
    "_handoff": "A cross-area blocker opens an issue in the owner's area, assigned to them and carrying the goal label, so their own loop picks it up. See handoff.py.",
    "_handoff_kit_findings": "#1551: what happens when a run produces a finding about SIGMA ITSELF rather than about your project. Default (this key absent): it is NEVER filed on your board. Your board is the list of work YOUR product needs, and a tool that files issues about its own internals onto it is competing for your attention with a dependency's bugs -- while hiding the finding from the only people who could fix it. Instead the finding's full text is written to .sdlc/state/withheld/<goal>.md and one ledger note is raised ADDRESSED TO YOU, naming where it went; nothing is ever silently dropped. Detection is structural, not label-based: the plugin is installed OUTSIDE your tree, so a finding is treated as the kit's only when its evidence cites a path that (a) exists under the plugin's install path, (b) sits under one of the plugin's SIGNATURE directories -- skills/, hooks/, .claude-plugin/, the ones that exist to hold a plugin -- and (c) sits under a top-level directory your repo does not have. All three, because the kit also ships files under docs/, tests/, evals/, examples/, .github/ and, in some installs, a private package's directory, and those are names your repo may perfectly well use: without (b), \"there is no CI, add .github/workflows/ci.yml\" -- the most ordinary finding a young repo produces -- was taken off that repo's own board, because a young repo is exactly the one with no .github/ yet. TWO RESIDUALS, both stated rather than implied. In the safe direction, and it is now a wide one: a genuine finding about the kit that cites docs/ or tests/ is filed on YOUR board, exactly as everything was before this existed. In the other direction, narrowed but not gone: if you mean to create a file at exactly a kit path under skills/, hooks/ or .claude-plugin/ and do not yet have that directory, that one finding is still taken from you. A repo where the kit IS the project (Sigma itself, or a vendored copy inside your tree) is detected and files normally. Set to \"file-locally\" to switch the routing off and get the old behaviour; only that exact literal does, because nothing should reach the mode that writes to a board by misspelling something else. See upstream.py.",
    "_handoff_upstream_repo": "#1551, OPT-IN and empty by default: an \"owner/name\" repo that kit findings may be filed on instead of merely ledgered -- e.g. the plugin's own issue tracker, if you have write access to it and want your loop's findings to reach its maintainers. Gated on cross_repo's three-valued access check (one read-only `gh api repos/<slug>`, on the rare kit-finding path only): ONLY a measured `granted` writes, and `unknown` -- which is what a drifted `gh` account produces -- is never a soft `granted`, so a misconfigured machine cannot file into a stranger's repository through this path. Anything other than `granted` degrades to the ledger, losing nothing. BEFORE YOU TURN THIS ON, KNOW EXACTLY WHAT LEAVES. The wrapper Sigma writes adds no identifier of yours, and the finding's MECHANICAL identifiers are stripped automatically -- your repo slug and owner, links other than ones pointing at the destination repo, absolute local paths, `@handles`, and the originating goal id -- on top of the usual secret scrubbing (which fails CLOSED: if it cannot run, nothing is published). But the finding's own PROSE is forwarded as its author wrote it, and prose is not de-identifiable: a sentence like \"our workaround in the payments lib\" still describes your project to anyone who knows it. The issue says so on its face. Enable this only where that is acceptable; leave it unset and the finding reaches you through the ledger instead, which loses nothing. Your own ledger note names the upstream issue either way, so you can still follow it up. Absent, or not an owner/name pair: nothing is asked and nothing is filed anywhere but the ledger.",
    "branch": "sdlc-ledger",
    "remote": "origin",
    "_branch": "The ops branch the ledger is shared on. .sdlc/ledger/ is a WORKTREE checked out to it (turn `enabled` on, then run /sigma-ledger — or `sync.py bootstrap` — once per clone to create+seed+push it), so pulling the ledger never touches your code checkout. It is never merged into the integration branch and never runs CI - keep it unprotected. Gitignore .sdlc/ledger/ on the code branch.",
    "receipt_sharing": false,
    "_receipt_sharing": "NOT YET SUPPORTED: true is refused with a warning and runs as false until #2680 makes receipt sharing work against real git. OPT-IN immutable ownership/merge receipt authority for merge observations. false (default) keeps receipts local and makes repository-wide rollout acceptance unavailable. true requires receipt_authority.trusted_signers to list the exact full signing fingerprints trusted to introduce receipt commits; a missing, malformed, untrusted, or unverifiable signature leaves the receipt pending and refuses shared observation.",
    "receipt_authority": { "trusted_signers": [] },
    "_receipt_authority": "The full 40-hex OpenPGP signing fingerprints allowed to introduce immutable receipt objects on the ledger branch. Empty is deliberately unusable when receipt_sharing is true; short key IDs, fingerprints of the wrong length, and unknown signatures are refused.",
    "watch": { "interval_seconds": 900, "log_max_bytes": 1048576 },
    "_watch": "interval_seconds: how often watch_daemon.py pulls the ops branch and refreshes .sdlc/state/inbox.md, which `loop.py next` surfaces between goals. log_max_bytes (#2499): the cap on .sdlc/state/watch.log -- a positive integer, anything else reads as the 1 MiB default. At the start of a tick the watcher rolls a log at or over the cap to exactly ONE predecessor, watch.log.1 (one atomic rename; an older .1 is replaced, there is never a .2), so follow it with `tail -F`, not `tail -f`. The effective cap is DERIVED from the machine: min(this value, 1% of the state volume's free space), and the disk clamp never drives it below 64 KiB unless you set it lower yourself. Disk is bounded at about 2 x cap plus whatever one tick writes after the check (observed at most ~7 KB in a broken tick; not bounded), i.e. ~2 MiB at the default, independent of interval and repo count. What scales with load is RETENTION, not disk -- arithmetic from measured tick sizes, not a timed run: an idle tick is ~76 B, so at 900 s the default holds ~13,800 ticks ~= 144 days per file (~288 d with .1); a broken tick (~2 KB, every sub-call failing) ~5 days; 10x the write rate (interval 90) ~14 days; 100x (interval 9) ~1.4 days. /sigma-doctor's `ledger watcher` row names the log's size once it reaches twice the cap with a fresh heartbeat -- the sign of an old-plugin watcher or a rotation that keeps failing.",
    "_publish_on_write": "Default ON, and absent here because there is nothing to turn on. A ledger write you TYPE (`ledger.py append`, `handoff.py open`/`track`/`ack`) publishes itself to the ops branch and says on stderr whether the team can see it -- the watcher above is the only OTHER thing that publishes, and it starts only from a loop trigger, so on a machine that is not looping those commands were the only chance the entry had. It never changes what the command returns: the entry is already on disk, so a failed push is a warning, never a lost write or a different exit code. If a watcher is already live the write names the tick that will carry it instead of racing it for the worktree index lock. Set \"publish_on_write\": false to keep writes local and leave every push to the watcher -- worth it on a slow or offline remote, at the cost of your notes being invisible to the team until a watcher tick lands.",
    "lease": { "ttl_hours": 12 },
    "_lease": "Claim lease: the loop skips a goal another actor holds an open `claimed` on (not yet done/parked/failed), so two people on one board don't start the same goal; a goal YOU hold is still yours to resume. Advisory (it sees claims already pulled to the ops branch, not a distributed lock), so keep assignee routing on too. ttl_hours expires a crashed claimer's hold so it can't block the team forever; 0 = never expire.",
    "autowatch": {
      "enabled": false,
      "scope": ["mentions", "assignments", "blockers"],
      "target_issue_only": true,
      "surface": "desktop",
      "poll_interval_minutes": 10,
      "channel_webhook_url": null,
      "allow_remote_webhook": false,
      "preconditions": {
        "no_concurrent_session": true,
        "load_average_ceiling": null,
        "spend_ceiling_tokens_per_week": null,
        "require_gh_auth": true
      },
      "hop_limit": 1
    },
    "_autowatch": "OPT-IN ledger-triggered autonomous tick (#1318 epic; the shared core that reads this block is autowatch.py, #1319). Needs ledger.enabled too, since it is watch_daemon.py's own tick that runs the check, mirroring agent_watch/comment_watch's convention above. When enabled:true, each tick checks ledger.addressed_to(me) for anything NEW and unacked matching `scope` (mentions/assignments/blockers); nothing found is a no-op. target_issue_only:true (the only value implemented) means a hit drives /sigma-loop scoped to that ONE flagged issue -- never an open-ended next-batch pick of the whole backlog. Before driving anything, every entry under `preconditions` is checked, short-circuiting on the first failure, cheapest first: require_gh_auth (`gh auth status`) -> no_concurrent_session (no other live session already working this dir) -> load_average_ceiling (a number, or null to skip the check entirely) -> spend_ceiling_tokens_per_week (checked against a LOCAL heuristic spend estimate that cannot see your own interactive usage or any other surface -- an approximation, never a true balance check; null skips it too). Any blocked precondition is logged to the ledger, never silently swallowed -- every tick leaves a trace on every outcome. hop_limit caps how many autowatch-triggered runs may chain from one original trigger (walked via the entry's own `autowatch_hop` field), so a run's own ledger writes can't cascade into an unbounded run of runs; default 1 means an autowatch-driven run's entries never arm a second hop. poll_interval_minutes is how often the adapter (Desktop scheduled task, or the CLI/Channels watcher) ticks; channel_webhook_url is optional and adapter-specific (null = no outbound notification configured) and accepts only http(s) loopback hosts unless allow_remote_webhook is exactly true. `surface` (#1323 revision) is \"desktop\" (default) or \"cli\" -- which adapter THIS machine runs; it's a DECLARED choice, not runtime-detected (there is no documented signal that tells a running session apart Desktop from CLI), so a team shares one committed value here and an individual machine overrides it locally with the SIGMA_AUTOWATCH_SURFACE env var (same per-machine-override idiom as SIGMA_AUTOWATCH_CMD) without needing its own config file. loop.py's own `start` nudges once, per the resolved surface, whenever autowatch is enabled but neither adapter is actually wired up yet (a channel_webhook_url set, or a Desktop scheduled task referencing autowatch.py) -- see doctor.py features/check for the same signal on demand. Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, is provably a no-op -- zero ledger writes, zero /sigma-loop invocation, same as every other opt-in feature's own fail-open contract."
  },
  "agent_watch": { "enabled": false, "notify": { "email": { "enabled": false, "host": "", "port": 587, "to": "", "user": "", "pass_env": "SIGMA_SMTP_PASS" } } },
  "_agent_watch": "OPT-IN background-agent-death watch. When enabled:true (needs ledger.enabled too - watch_daemon.py's own tick is what runs the check), each tick checks every goal with an open ledger claim for a registered `(goal, thread)` marker (loop.py agent-start/agent-end) whose pid has genuinely died, and notifies once per dead pid. notify.email is stdlib smtplib only - zero new dependency - and NEVER accepts a literal password: pass_env names an environment variable (default SIGMA_SMTP_PASS) holding it, since this file is git-committed. A misconfigured or failing send is loud on stderr and always falls back to a ledger note addressed to the goal's claimant - never silently drops a notification. Off by default; absent, nothing registers or checks a marker.",
  "comment_watch": { "enabled": false },
  "_comment_watch": "OPT-IN comment-to-ledger claimant notification (#385). When enabled:true (needs ledger.enabled + a github discovery.source too - watch_daemon.py's own tick is what runs the check), each tick fetches comments for every issue with an open ledger claim and writes one scrubbed ledger note per new comment, addressed to the claimant, surfaced in their next inbox exactly like agent_watch's own notifications. Self-commenting is suppressed; a second tick over the same comment writes nothing. Off by default; absent, nothing is fetched or written.",
  "drift_watch": { "enabled": false, "ttl_minutes": 90, "channels": { "sigma": null, "org": null }, "slack_bot_token_env": "SIGMA_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
  "_drift_watch": "OPT-IN passive feature-branch drift watcher + Slack post (#2311, epic #2310, design #2289). When enabled:true, EACH tick threaded into watch_daemon.py checks, at most once every ttl_minutes (default 90 - coarser than watch_daemon.py's own 15-minute tick, mirroring discovery.reconcile.ttl_minutes's own idiom), every OPEN unit declared in .sdlc/features/ for two read-only signals: how many commits the integration branch carries that its feature/<name> branch does not (feature_rebase.landed_commits, reused directly), and whether that branch's own landing pull request is open, closed, merged, or missing (unit_completion._landing_pull_requests, reused directly). Nothing here moves a branch, opens a PR, or writes a label - strictly informational. NEEDS ledger.enabled: true TOO, and not incidentally the way agent_watch/comment_watch/autowatch do above: this feature's own dedup mechanism is built entirely on the shared ledger (a summary the WHOLE TEAM sees in one Slack channel cannot be deduped by a private per-machine cursor the way channel_notify.py's own personal-nudge cursor can), so enabled(config) refuses to run at all while ledger.enabled stays off or unset, however drift_watch.enabled itself got set. channels.sigma and channels.org are two DIFFERENT hardcoded Slack channel ids - fill in exactly ONE for your repo (sigma's own config points at its own channel; every other repo in an org points at a shared one instead); leaving both null means the tick still runs and logs what it found, but posts nothing anywhere. slack_bot_token_env NAMES an environment variable (default SIGMA_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN) holding the bot token - NEVER a literal value here, since this file is git-committed; an empty/unset token degrades to a logged [stub] line rather than failing. Off by default; absent, no git or gh call is made and nothing is posted.",
  "slack_commands": { "enabled": false, "channel_id": null, "app_token_env": "SIGMA_SLACK_BOT_SOCKET_TOKEN", "bot_token_env": "SIGMA_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN" },
  "_slack_commands": "OPT-IN inbound Slack commands listener (#2336, slice 1 of epic #2335, design #2329). A separate, standalone, long-running process (skills/sigma-loop/scripts/slack_commands_listen.py) holds one Slack Socket Mode connection open and answers five structured commands typed into ONE curated channel: --drift, --merge <name>, --list [page], --rebase <name>, --help. Not tick-based like every other watcher in this file -- it is started once, manually, by a human or a supervisor (see skills/sigma-loop/SLACK_COMMANDS.md for the one-time Socket Mode + App-Level Token setup and the pip install this needs), and does not ride watch_daemon.py's own tick. channel_id is the ONE Slack channel id commands are accepted from -- this is the WHOLE authorization model (decision #2 on #2329): a command is authorized if and only if it arrived on this exact channel id, never a per-user allowlist and never merely 'the token was valid'; leaving it null means the listener refuses to start at all (enabled(config) reads channel_id too, not just enabled:true). app_token_env and bot_token_env NAME two environment variables (pass_env convention, matching drift_watch.slack_bot_token_env above) -- NEVER literal values here: app_token_env holds the App-Level Token (connections:write scope, what Socket Mode itself authenticates with) and bot_token_env holds the bot token used to post replies -- the SAME token drift_watch already uses (SIGMA_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN) and the SAME Slack App, with Socket Mode added to it rather than a second, dedicated app (the real deployed setup; design #2329's original decision #4/Doubt D-6 called for a separate app instead). Off by default; absent, or enabled:false, or channel_id unset, is provably a no-op -- the listener refuses to start, same as every other opt-in feature's own fail-open contract.",
  "journal": {
    "enabled": false,
    "_comment": "OPT-IN local activity journal (#2574). Sigma's own record of what the loop DID -- phase timings, gate verdicts, verify results, counts and durations; never code, never diffs, never prose bodies. Written to .sdlc/events/<actor>-<host>.<pid>.jsonl, one file per writing process (the same fan-out as the entries stream; the whole journal is the union of every *.jsonl in that directory, so many files is normal). LOCAL AND GITIGNORED, and NEVER PUBLISHED: nothing stages it to a branch, no teammate pulls it, and git is not asked to hold it. OFF BY DEFAULT -- with enabled:false, or this block absent, zero journal bytes are written and neither .sdlc/events/ nor .sdlc/ledger/events/ is created; a permanent test (tests/test_core_only_writes.py) proves it against a fresh /sigma-init. Turn it on by setting enabled:true here -- the JSON boolean true, checked with `is True`, so \"true\" and 1 do nothing. An organisation's .sdlc/managed-settings.json may also lock journal.enabled, and a lock beats this key either way: a locked false wins over a local true, and a lock whose refreshed_at is older than 7 days turns the journal off until it is refreshed. Files older than 30 days are deleted on a process's first write, whether or not the journal is on, so nothing here grows without bound. /sigma-doctor features reports the live state, including which of your config and the managed settings turned it on."
  },
  "action_log": { "enabled": true },
  "_action_log": "Local-only action trace — every file touched, model/effort choice, subagent dispatch and mechanically-guaranteed loop action (claim/worktree/verify/gate/record), written to .sdlc/state/log/<goal-stem>.jsonl (gitignored, per RUNTIME_IGNORES; never the ledger, never shared). #2741: ships ON directly from /sigma-init as of 1.0.0 (was off) -- a fresh install gets the audit trail from the first goal. Set enabled:false to opt back out; loop.py log and every internal call site are then no-ops, exactly as before this change. Read with /sigma-log.",
  "parallel": { "enabled": false, "max_concurrent": 3, "goals": { "enabled": false, "max_concurrent": 3 } },
  "_parallel": "Run a goal's independent slices concurrently, from the declared manifest .sdlc/plans/<goal-stem>.slices.json: `slices.py plan` groups non-conflicting slices into waves (a slice that declares no files conflicts with everything and runs alone) and the loop dispatches each wave as subagents. Off by default; absent, every goal runs as one unit exactly as before.",
  "_parallel_goals": "Sibling knob, independent of parallel.enabled above: run MULTIPLE BACKLOG GOALS concurrently (F10.5-3/#375), not one goal's slices. goals.enabled:true makes /sigma-loop's `next-batch` return up to goals.max_concurrent goals in one pass, each dispatched as its own subagent running the full research-through-PR sequence in its own worktree+branch. Off by default; absent or enabled:false, `next-batch` returns exactly one goal, byte-identical to plain `next`. A bad/non-numeric max_concurrent falls back to 3 rather than raising. The target scenario is ONE person, ONE machine, many of their OWN assigned issues - not a team coordination feature (that's what the ledger + #374's claim check are for). Refilling a freed slot MUST pass `next --skip <the other still-live goals>`, or a sibling's in-progress goal can be re-dispatched - see the SKILL.md prose (step 1a) for why the claim check alone cannot catch this.",

  "backlog_check": { "enabled": false, "action": "park", "similarity": "tfidf", "dup_threshold": 0.72, "obsolete_threshold": 0.72, "park_threshold": 0.80, "top_k": 8, "closed_window_days": "auto", "embed": { "enabled": false, "command": "", "weight": 0.5 }, "mirror": { "ttl_minutes": 60, "closed_limit": 200 } },
  "_backlog_check": "OPT-IN pre-work cross-check (backlog_check.py + mirror.py, driven by /sigma-loop's precheck step). When enabled:true, before a picked goal spends a token the loop retrieves likely DUPLICATE / OBSOLETE-by-completed-work / BLOCKED-BY items from the rest of the backlog (+ the team ledger) via a token-free TF-IDF pass; a CONFIDENT hit is parked-with-proof (a comment carrying the evidence, then the loop advances to the next goal), a weak one is only annotated. Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, changes nothing. Zero LLM cost, secret-safe (findings carry issue refs + scrubbed shared terms, never titles/bodies), fail-open (never blocks the loop). action: park (default, park a confident hit) | flag (only ever annotate, never park). similarity: tfidf (default) | bm25 (BM25-weighted cosine, stronger on long bodies). dup_threshold / obsolete_threshold: cosine 0-1 at which an OPEN / a COMPLETED issue is a candidate. park_threshold: at/above this a finding is CONFIDENT (parks) - keep park_threshold >= dup_threshold or every candidate parks. top_k: max candidates scored per goal. closed_window_days: 'auto' scales the obsolescence window to ~50 recent merges by git velocity; a pinned integer overrides. embed: OPT-IN dense/embedding layer fused with the lexical score (max of the two) to catch PARAPHRASES that share no words. embed.enabled:true + embed.command = a provider-agnostic embedder (reads text on stdin, prints a JSON number array). embed.command runs via the shell, same trust model as verify.command - it is YOUR command. Vectors are cached in gitignored .sdlc/state/embeddings.json (keyed by embedder identity + content, so changing embed.command self-invalidates; only new/changed texts are embedded), so only the FIRST run over a new issue pays the embed cost and the query itself stays token-free. embed.weight scales the dense score in the fusion. Fail-open: no command / an embedder error -> lexical-only (degraded:['no_embedder']). mirror.ttl_minutes: reuse the gitignored board snapshot this long before refetching (github mode). mirror.closed_limit: how many recently-closed issues to scan for obsolescence.",

  "goal_decompose": { "enabled": false, "mode": "log", "max_children": 8 },
  "_goal_decompose": "OPT-IN oversized-goal classifier (goal_size.py + loop.py's decompose-check verb, driven by /sigma-loop's own pick-path, mirroring backlog_check's shape above). Before a picked goal spends a token, a deterministic zero-LLM classifier (body word/line count, independent ## sections, top-level checkboxes, explicit multi-phase structure) flags a goal that reads like an epic wearing one issue number; a goal already marked as a decomposition child/meta-goal is exempt by construction. log/park never file anything; file additionally files a tracked meta-issue (see below) - either way the actual decomposition (drafting child goals) always runs LATER as its own normal SDLC goal, protected by the same plan-review/budget/claims machinery every goal already gets, never inline in this check. mode: log (default once enabled; classify + annotate via the local action log, ZERO mutation ever) | park (park a flagged goal for a human to split) | file (park AND file ONE idempotency-guarded \"Decompose #N\" meta-issue via handoff.create_tracked_issue first - a strict read of the parent's own comments for a prior sigma:decompose-filed marker means a re-run that gets as far as the marker can never double-file; a backlog source with no issue-creation seam at all, e.g. local goal files, degrades honestly to the same park action). max_children: caps how many child goals the filed meta-goal's own later decomposition may create - interpolated straight into that meta-goal's own instructions. The classifier's own thresholds (body length, section count, checkbox count) are corpus-calibrated - fit against 269 of THIS repo's own issues (measured 2026-08-08), not inline fixtures alone; see goal_size.py's module docstring for the both-direction margins. A new adopter's issue conventions may still differ from this repo's, so mode log remains the recommended first rung until you've seen what the classifier would have flagged on your OWN real goals - a miscalibrated threshold in a different repo then only ever annotates instead of parking a goal that didn't need it. Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, changes nothing.",

  "goal_design": { "enabled": false, "mode": "full" },
  "_goal_design": "OPT-IN undesigned-goal RETROFIT gate (loop.py's design-check verb, driven by /sigma-loop's own pick-path, placed directly after decompose-check - docs/dossier-pipeline.md §6, 'the retrofit gate'). Before a picked goal spends a token, unless it already carries the sdlc:designed label, park it and file ONE idempotency-guarded \"Design #N\" meta-issue instructing a codebase-mapping design pass (skills/sigma-goal-design/SKILL.md) - the coverage mechanism that closes the gap goal_decompose's own classifier leaves open (it measures SIZE, never BLAST RADIUS): nothing skips the mapping step just by being born outside the Dossier/Product-path front door (sigma-dossier -> goal-design -> goal-review, the OTHER path onto this same label). A goal already marked as a decompose/design bookkeeping meta-goal is exempt by construction, same shape decompose-check already uses. mode: full (default once enabled - the contract's own stated default depth, §6a; run the comprehensive blast-radius sweep) | lane (a cheaper, lane-sized check for smaller retrofit goals, reusing discovery.py's own small/medium/large lane concept). Unlike goal_decompose.mode's three values (log/park/file, which pick the ACTION), this axis never changes what design-check DOES - once enabled it always park-and-files - it only changes the DEPTH instructions rendered into the filed meta-issue's own body. sdlc:designed itself is written only once goal-review (contract §7) confirms a design - not goal-design alone, so an interruption between the two re-runs design rather than silently skipping review. Turning this on RETROFITS THE ENTIRE LIVE BACKLOG (every open sdlc:goal issue lacking sdlc:designed) on the very next pick - a deliberate, standalone decision, not something to flip casually. Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, changes nothing. rounds: OPTIONAL positive integer (#2032), raises the mode:full sweep-round ceiling above its unconfigured default of 3 - inert under mode:lane, where the ceiling stays fixed at one round per measured lane (small=1/medium=2/large=3). Absent changes nothing; there is no unbounded value, since every round is real Anthropic spend.",

  "spend_approval": { "enabled": false, "approvers": [], "classes": ["spend"] },
  "_spend_approval": "OPT-IN, per-goal, single-use exemption from the loop's park-on-an-irreversible-or-expensive-action rule (#722, Part 1 only; loop.py's spend-approval verb, spend_approval.py). Before parking on such a step the agent runs `loop.py spend-approval <dir> <goal> --action \"<step>\"`; it answers APPROVED (exit 0) only when ALL hold: enabled is exactly true; the goal's issue BODY has `sigma:spend-approved=<label>` as its FIRST LINE, exactly, nothing else on that line (never a comment, never a later line - markdown can render any later line as code or hide it); the issue's AUTHOR login is in approvers (case-insensitive; empty or malformed list = nobody can authorise) AND GitHub reports the author as OWNER, MEMBER or COLLABORATOR (sources.TRUSTED_ASSOCIATIONS; anything else is DENIED); that label has not been used before (single use - write a NEW label to authorise again); and a durable audit comment (marker, author, the action, body hash, time) posted successfully first. Anything else is DENIED (exit 3) or OFF (exit 4) and the goal parks exactly as before. LIMITS, stated: this is a boolean go-ahead, NOT a dollar cap - nothing meters external spend (a GPU host, a video API) and budget.max_tokens only meters the loop's own tokens (a real cap is a separate follow-up); it authenticates 'an approver opened this issue' (any repo writer can still add the marker to such an issue, since the check is on who OPENED it, not who last edited it; approvers only narrows who may open one; an editor check is a follow-up) , not 'a human typed this line' (a loop running under the operator's own login could append the marker itself - the audit comment is how you would see that); and it cannot stop an agent that never asks. Needs a GitHub issue backlog (a local backlog is always DENIED). Off by default: absent, or enabled:false, changes nothing. `classes` (default [\"spend\"], PROVISIONAL) lists the action classes a recorded permission may clear; it can only narrow, because a class other than spend is refused until a human-only signal exists, and `--class <name>` on the verb names the class of the step. The marker may carry an optional digest, `sigma:spend-approved=<label>:<12 hex>`, the first 12 hex of the sha256 of the step text with whitespace collapsed; a step whose text differs is DENIED. Each use is also counted in the gitignored state file `spend-approval-used.json`, so deleting the audit comment cannot re-arm a label.",

  "context": { "documents": [], "inline_max_bytes": 16384 },
  "_context": "Reference documents for the reviewer brief (review_context.py). documents: repo-relative paths added after the built-in north-star and project documents; each is read only if it resolves inside the repository (a parent-escaping, absolute or linked-out path is refused with a reason), and an absent one is stated, never skipped silently. Leave the key out entirely and the brief is exactly what it was; an explicit empty list adds a line saying no documents were named. inline_max_bytes: a document larger than this is given as a pointer instead of inlined (default 16384).",
  "review": { "independent": true, "host": "auto", "command": "", "timeout_seconds": 900 },
  "_review": "The maker should not be the checker, and the code cannot prove it was not. Every LLM-judgment review gate (plan-review, code review, the post-PR review, and the retrospective grade) is asked to run as a FRESH subagent that gets the PROJECT but not the AUTHOR - so it can find blast radius and disagree instead of rubber-stamping its own work (which lower-tier models do, amplifying hallucination). The reviewer brief is assembled by review_context.py (north-star + conventions + contracts + the goal + a pointer to the artifact - always the whole project, never just the diff and never the maker's transcript); /sigma-loop is told to dispatch one fresh subagent per gate fed only that brief.",
  "_review_independent": "true (default) = ask for a fresh author-blind reviewer subagent per gate, grounded in the north-star + CLAUDE.md + contracts + full repo READ access so it judges the change's ripple through callers, not just the diff on screen (a diff-only reviewer cannot see what a small change breaks two files away). Where the host has no subagents it degrades honestly to an inline reviewer that still loads the brief fresh and takes the reviewer's stance. false = legacy inline behavior (the maker reviews its own work) - only for a trivial solo repo where the ceremony isn't worth it.",
  "_review_host": "WHICH MECHANISM gives this host an author-blind reviewer. Independence is a property of how the review is RUN, not a claim the reviewer can make about itself - so each host reaches it by its own best route and NO BRANCH IS A DOWNGRADE. auto (default) = detect: review.command if set, else a Claude marker -> a fresh subagent, else a Codex session marker -> a fresh read-only codex exec process when its CLI is available. claude = force the subagent path. cursor = spawn `cursor-agent -p --output-format text`. codex = a fresh read-only `codex exec --ephemeral` process; review verdict quality still needs an end-to-end gate check on the target host. command = run review.command (reads the brief on stdin, prints the review; same trust model as verify.command - it is YOUR command). inline = the reviewer is the author, and the verdict must say so. Auto-detection recognizes observed Claude and Codex session markers; Cursor still needs review.host because its marker has not been verified. A named host whose CLI is absent resolves inline NAMING THE MISSING BINARY, never a silent self-review. timeout_seconds (default 900) bounds a spawned reviewer: a real review takes minutes, and a subprocess with no timeout has already hung a pick here (cross_repo.py:225).",

  "companions": "auto",
  "model_selection": "off",
  "_model_selection_options": "off (portable prediction disabled; inline work uses the session model, while a dispatched Codex phase maps off to its versioned ordinary-work model) | auto (predict a tier per goal via /sigma-model, run that goal's phases as subagents at it)",
  "model_selection_max_tier": "opus",
  "_model_selection_max_tier": "PRICE CEILING on the tier the router may resolve (#2564). The router resolves conflicts UPWARD because over-powering a mislabelled goal is assumed cheaper than under-powering a hard one -- true of opus at 2.5x sonnet, and FALSE of fable, which is priced at 5x ($10/$50 per 1M vs sonnet's $2/$10). So a single creative stem (blog, vision, tagline, narrativ, storytell, prose, marketing copy, creative writing) anywhere in a goal's title OR body promoted even a typo fix to the most expensive tier, and under model_selection:auto the resolved tier is the ceiling for EVERY phase of that goal -- an unattended overnight run exhausted an account's credits exactly this way. Defaults to \"opus\", so out of the box no goal text can resolve to fable. When the winning tier is priced above this ceiling the TIER clamps to min(sonnet, ceiling) and THE SIGNAL THAT FIRED IS STILL REPORTED, so `predict.py why \"<goal>\"` still shows you which literal fired and model_selection_signal_excludes below stays usable. Set to \"fable\" if your repo's work genuinely IS creative writing; \"haiku\" or \"sonnet\" also bind the DEFAULT tier, so they run everything at or below that tier including goals the router rated opus -- cheap, and silent about it. Only the four literals haiku|sonnet|opus|fable are honored (case-insensitive, stripped); anything else is a typo and falls back to \"opus\" rather than silently uncapping the router. Honored by /sigma-loop's per-goal and per-step resolution and by /sigma-model's own output. NOTE this is NOT a spend limit: it removes fable's 5x multiplier, it does not bound a run's total cost -- budget.max_tokens above counts tokens, not price.",
  "model_selection_signal_excludes": [],
  "_model_selection_signal_excludes": "OPT-IN per-repo escape hatch for the router's keyword signals (#1601). The tier heuristic is a fixed regex over the goal text, and some of its signals are creative terms on most repos but ordinary DOMAIN NOUNS on yours -- a storytelling product's \"narrative stage\" or \"prose renderer\", and \"vision\" on any repo that uses Sigma's own vision-first north-star vocabulary -- so plain engineering goals route to the creative (fable) tier. Empty by default: the router behaves exactly as it always has. List the SIGNALS to neutralize here, not whole tiers and not phrases: run `predict.py why \"<a goal that misrouted>\"` and copy the literal it prints after `signal=` (e.g. \"narrativ\", \"vision\", \"prose\"). Matching is case-insensitive and either-direction, so the natural word (\"narrative\") works as well as the router's stem, and one stem covers the prefixed forms a pattern admits. An excluded signal falls through to the remaining signals and tiers, so the tier's OTHER signals still fire -- a genuinely creative goal on your repo still reaches the creative tier via storytell/blog/marketing copy/tagline. NOTE (#2564): actually RESOLVING to fable additionally requires model_selection_max_tier above to be opted up to \"fable\"; on the default \"opus\" ceiling that tier is unreachable whatever this list says. The two knobs are independent -- this one names domain vocabulary, that one names a price ceiling. The cost, deliberately accepted: an excluded signal is excluded repo-wide, including where you DID mean it creatively. Honored by /sigma-loop's per-goal and per-step resolution and by /sigma-model's own output; malformed values are ignored rather than raising.",
  "model_host_overrides": {},
  "_model_host_overrides": "Optional host-specific substitutions for /sigma-model's portable tier resolver. Choose only this plugin release's approved Codex IDs: gpt-5.5, gpt-5.6-luna, gpt-5.6-sol, gpt-5.6-terra, or gpt-6-astra; for example {\"codex\": {\"sonnet\": {\"model\": \"gpt-5.6-terra\", \"effort\": \"medium\"}}}. A changed host catalog requires a plugin update, never an arbitrary or Claude model string. Every supplied mapping has exactly model and effort; effort is low, medium, or high. That effort is only the legacy tier-only fallback: a dispatch with Sigma's canonical selected effort preserves the selected value. Empty is the shipped mapping. A malformed Codex mapping refuses before dispatch rather than guessing or sending the portable tier as a model ID.",
  "knowledge_graph": {
    "enabled": false,
    "_comment": "Opt-in research corpus. When enabled, the research-capture hook writes a provenance breadcrumb per WebSearch/WebFetch under .sdlc/knowledge/research/web/ — source + subject + a SHORT excerpt scrubbed of secret-shaped substrings, never the raw page. .sdlc/knowledge/ is gitignored by /sigma-setup so captures stay local unless you deliberately commit them.",
    "scope": "full",
    "_scope_options": "full (code + external research + internal analysis) | research (skip code; internal analysis + external research only)",
    "builder": "graphify",
    "auto_refresh": false,
    "web_retention_enabled": false,
    "web_retention_days": 90,
    "web_retention_disk_share": 0.0001,
    "_web_retention": "Opt-in bound on the local web captures. When web_retention_enabled is true, every recorded goal (the same hop as auto_refresh, independent of it) archives captures older than web_retention_days (0 = age bound off) and, oldest first, enough to bring the set under a byte bound DERIVED from this machine: web_retention_disk_share of the total size of the disk holding .sdlc/knowledge (0.0001 = 49 MB on a 494 GB disk, 10 MB on 100 GB), measured as disk occupancy. Archive, never delete: files move to .sdlc/knowledge/archive/research/web/ with the same names; the count is reported; a second pass moves nothing. A malformed value refuses and moves nothing. Hand run: kg.py retain .sdlc (the verb is the opt-in). Shared analysis notes are never trimmed automatically - kg.py maintain prints the command, you run it.",
    "sync": {
      "enabled": false,
      "branch": "sdlc-knowledge",
      "remote": "origin"
    },
    "_sync": "Opt-in sharing of ANALYSIS NOTES across machines, on the ledger's ops-branch pattern. What leaves the machine: ONLY .sdlc/knowledge/analysis/**/*.md (the notes Retrospective writes) — never research/, gaps.md, a graph build, or any non-.md file beside the notes; the worktree's own .gitignore enforces this, prose does not. When enabled (strict true, together with knowledge_graph.enabled), `loop.py record ... --retro-grade` publishes this machine's notes to <remote>/<branch> and pulls everyone else's before the graph refresh. Publish REFUSES a note containing a secret-shaped string, naming the file (it inspects what it is about to stage; a note committed by hand inside the worktree is already past the gate). One-time per clone: `python3 <plugin>/skills/sigma-loop/scripts/sync.py bootstrap .sdlc --channel knowledge` (carries existing local notes in). Manual lever after a failed push: the same command with `publish`."
  }
}
